item.content:encoded:
Today, we’re excited to announce the release of .NET 11! This year’s release 
.NET 11 is a Standard Term Support (STS) release with  under the .NET support policy. STS and Long Term Support (LTS) releases have the same quality and servicing policies; the difference is the length of support. .NET 10 remains a supported LTS option. Stay current with servicing updates whichever release you choose.
Join us at .NET Conf, November 10-13Explore .NET 11 with the product teams and community at .NET Conf 2026. The conference includes product team sessions on November 10 and 11, followed by two days of community sessions.Some of the changes we’re most excited for you to try:C# 15 union types and closed hierarchies help you express the possible states of your application and handle them with compiler-checked patterns.A Native AOT CLI host and a warm MSBuild server reduce overhead in everyday developer workflows.ASP.NET Core brings asynchronous validation, OpenAPI 3.2, and new capabilities for Blazor and SignalR applications. Experimental Blazor AI components integrate with the Agent User Interaction Protocol (AG-UI) for advanced agentic web experiences.Build AI-powered, agentic applications with Microsoft.Extensions.AI (MEAI) for model access, MCP for tool integration, and Microsoft Agent Framework (MAF) for agents and workflows.Put agentic development tools to work with .NET Skills for .NET-specific guidance, .NET MAUI DevFlow for inspecting and testing running apps, and the .NET Upgrade canvas for reviewing modernization progress..NET MAUI adopts CoreCLR as its default runtime and improves XAML iteration and native controls.The libraries add built-in Zstandard compression, simpler process execution, richer JSON support, and new ways to work with in-memory data without copying it first.C# 15 is the default language version for projects targeting .NET 11. Union types are one of its biggest additions: a value can be one of a declared set of case types, with conversions from those types and exhaustiveness checking in switch expressions.Consider a product lookup that either finds a product or reports that it wasn’t found:ProductLookup result = new Product(42, "Mechanical keyboard");

string message = result switch
{
    Product product => $"Found {product.Name}",
    ProductNotFound missing => $"No product with ID {missing.Id}",
};

Console.WriteLine(message);

public sealed record Product(int Id, string Name);
public sealed record ProductNotFound(int Id);
public union ProductLookup(Product, ProductNotFound);The result type tells a caller which outcomes it needs to handle. There’s no need to use  or an unrelated base class just to put those outcomes behind one return type.C# 15 also adds . A  class limits direct derivation to its declaring assembly, giving the compiler the information it needs to check patterns over the hierarchy. Unions are useful when you want to combine existing types; closed hierarchies fit models where you own the inheritance relationship.Smaller language features help with everyday code, too. Collection expression arguments let you pass constructor or factory arguments without giving up collection expression syntax:HashSet<string> names =
    [with(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase), "Alice", "ALICE", "Bob"];

Console.WriteLine(names.Count);This prints : the comparer controls equality, so  and  are one entry.Other additions include extension indexers and labeled  and . These features don’t require the preview language version. The separate memory safety work ; see the C# 15 feature documentation for its opt-in requirements.F# 11 improves compiler memory use and reduces allocations in FSharp.Core collection operations. It also adds ways to bound concurrent asynchronous work, useful when a collection operation would otherwise start more work than a service or machine can handle comfortably.The F# release notes distinguish the default-language updates from preview capabilities, including Runtime Async support for custom computation-expression builders.The SDK now enables a Native AOT CLI host by default. Commands such as help and version queries can run without starting the managed CLI, and tool launches can avoid that startup path too. Commands that need managed execution still use it.The MSBuild server also stays warm between CLI invocations by default, reducing repeated startup work. SDK installers on Linux and macOS are smaller through assembly deduplication.File-based apps can grow across source files with , and solution filters can be created and edited from the CLI.  adds Aspire app-host integration, crash recovery, and device selection for mobile projects.For teams using Microsoft.Testing.Platform,  gains run-level timeouts, failure limits, clearer progress reporting, and per-module results directories. The test runner matters: these switches aren’t interchangeable with the VSTest command-line options.Container publishing adds reproducible image digests through  and avoids redundant layer uploads when the target manifest already exists. NuGet source configuration gains a minimum package publication age setting, which lets teams wait before newly published package versions become eligible for selection. This is an additional policy control, not a replacement for vulnerability checks or reviewing dependencies.Performance work in .NET 11 reaches both the code you deploy and the tools you use to develop it. Stephen Toub’s Performance Improvements in .NET 11 takes a detailed tour through hundreds of runtime and library optimizations, with benchmarks and code examples.The just-in-time (JIT) compiler improvements remove more redundant bounds checks and branches, improve devirtualization, and fold more operations into constants. For example, the JIT can optimize additional  calls when it knows the inputs at compile time. These changes improve generated code without requiring you to rewrite the corresponding application logic.Native AOT interface dispatch uses a shared helper that reduces the code needed at interface call sites and improves throughput for interface-heavy workloads. Hardware support also expands, including FP16 instructions for  operations on supported x64 and Arm64 hardware and new vector construction and composition APIs.There’s useful work on diagnostics, too. In-process crash report logging can capture a managed stack trace, module information, and runtime state before a process exits. Previously available on mobile platforms, this reporting path now also supports Linux and macOS.As always, measure the effect on your own application. Throughput, startup time, memory use, and deployment size depend on the workload and publishing options you choose.Runtime Async has been a recurring theme throughout the .NET 11 previews. It moves asynchronous suspension and resumption into the runtime instead of relying on compiler-generated state machines.One especially useful result is a clearer  call stack. Profilers and debuggers can show the asynchronous methods themselves instead of the infrastructure used to execute them. This is a distinction from exception stack traces, which already hide much of that infrastructure.Runtime Async remains a preview feature. To try it in a project targeting , add:<PropertyGroup>
  <Features>runtime-async=on</Features>
</PropertyGroup>You don’t need to enable  for this scenario. The .NET runtime libraries themselves are built with Runtime Async, but opting your application’s code into the new compilation model is a separate choice. See the Runtime Async documentation for its behavior and current guidance, and test your application’s dependencies and diagnostics before adopting it.The .NET 11 SDK includes an MCP server template. MCP gives AI applications a common way to discover and call tools and services. The template uses the official MCP C# SDK and includes a working sample tool, so you can start exposing your own functionality without first installing a separate template package:dotnet new mcpserver -o MyMcpServerFor applications that consume AI services, Microsoft.Extensions.AI (MEAI) provides abstractions such as  and . These libraries let you compose model access with dependency injection, caching, and OpenTelemetry using familiar .NET patterns. They’re available as NuGet packages, rather than features that require you to move all of your application code to a new framework version.Microsoft Agent Framework (MAF) builds on these abstractions with agents that call tools and maintain conversation state, plus workflows that coordinate agents and application code. Connect agents to MCP tools to give them access to your application’s capabilities, and expose an agent through an ASP.NET Core AG-UI endpoint to build an interactive Blazor experience. The AG-UI hosting integration is currently prerelease.The .NET libraries add capabilities that previously required extra setup, intermediate buffers, or custom helpers.The  API expansion includes one-shot methods to run a process and capture its output and exit status. More advanced APIs give you control over inherited handles, suspended starts, and process signaling. Build tools and developer utilities can spend less code on process plumbing while still inspecting how a child process finished. See Process API Improvements in .NET 11 for examples.Zstandard compression joins the existing compression formats in . New span-based compression APIs support callers that manage their own buffers, and ZIP improvements include CRC32 validation when reading entries.Four new in-memory stream adapters bridge existing data to APIs that accept a : , , , and . For example, a pipeline’s segmented  can be read as a stream without first flattening it into a new contiguous buffer. adds support for C# unions, F# discriminated unions, and JSON Lines output from asynchronous sequences. F# union support uses reflection and doesn’t support source generation, trimming, or Native AOT, so check the documented limits before choosing it for a trimmed application.LINQ adds , which preserves unmatched elements from both inputs. This is useful for reconciliation work, such as comparing two inventories where you need to see missing items on either side.The libraries also expand numeric types, DNS resolution, cryptography, and tracing configuration. The library overview covers the complete set of changes and marks APIs that remain experimental.EF Core removes unnecessary joins in more reference-navigation and split-query scenarios, improves  translation, and translates the new LINQ  operator on relational providers that support it. These changes can simplify generated SQL while letting more queries stay in LINQ.Complex types and JSON columns can now be used on entities mapped with table-per-type or table-per-concrete-type inheritance. Other improvements include migrations tooling and additional provider-specific JSON capabilities.For SQL Server applications, EF Core adds support for vector indexes and approximate vector search. The SQL Server features and corresponding EF APIs are experimental. See the vector search documentation and limitations before evaluating them.EF Core 11 requires .NET 11. When upgrading, check your database provider’s compatibility and the EF Core 11 breaking changes as well as the runtime changes.The new dotnetup toolchain manager lets you install and update .NET SDKs and runtimes in your user account. It’s currently in public preview and helps you manage side-by-side versions and keep up with servicing updates.The experimental .NET MAUI DevFlow toolkit exposes a running app’s visual tree, screenshots, and interaction tools, so an agent can inspect and test the UI rather than work only from source code.For modernization, the .NET Upgrade canvas gives you a visual view of a GitHub Copilot upgrade session. Review assessment findings and dependency compatibility, follow implementation tasks, and monitor failures and progress as the upgrade agent works. Our modernization walkthrough in the GitHub Copilot app covers the workflow from assessment through execution.Web: ASP.NET Core and BlazorApplication types are most useful when the rest of the stack understands them. ASP.NET Core supports C# unions wherever it uses , including JSON request and response bodies in Minimal APIs and MVC. Generated OpenAPI documents describe the union’s alternatives with an  schema.This support applies to JSON bodies, not to route, query, header, or form binding. When multiple union cases have the same JSON shape, configure a classifier so deserialization can distinguish them. The ASP.NET Core union documentation explains those boundaries.Validation also becomes more practical for rules that need to do I/O. Asynchronous validation works with the new asynchronous DataAnnotations APIs, so a validator can await a database lookup or a remote call instead of blocking a thread. Minimal APIs and Blazor forms can use this support.OpenAPI 3.2 is now the default for generated documents. Among other improvements, it lets the document describe the per-event payload of a server-sent event stream. If a client generator or another part of your toolchain doesn’t yet support 3.2, you can explicitly select an earlier document version.For long-lived connections, SignalR authentication refresh lets a connection refresh authentication without reconnecting. Blazor Interactive Server components can receive the updated identity and react when claims change.Blazor adds capabilities for both statically rendered pages and interactive applications.The new  component caches the rendered output of a component subtree during static server-side rendering. On a cache hit, Blazor replays the markup without creating those child components or running their lifecycle methods. That gives you a smaller unit of caching for expensive, mostly static sections of a page.Interactive Server apps can opt into automatic circuit pause when a browser tab becomes inactive. After a configurable delay, pausing frees the server memory and SignalR connection held by an inactive user. The feature requires an additional package and explicit configuration; upgrading doesn’t silently change your application’s circuit behavior.Forms gain client-side validation for static server rendering and asynchronous validation support.  and  add ways to move to a particular item, useful when returning a user to their place in a large collection.Blazor also adds components for agentic user interfaces. The new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.AI package includes components for streaming chat, tool rendering, approval flows, and shared UI state. Basic chat works with any . For advanced experiences with a remote agent, the AG-UI .NET integration carries streamed responses, tool events, approval requests, and shared-state updates between an ASP.NET Core agent endpoint and the Blazor UI.The Blazor AI components package is experimental and remains prerelease throughout .NET 11. It’s an opportunity to try the direction and give feedback, not a stable part of the ASP.NET Core shared framework. See the Blazor AI components documentation..NET MAUI in .NET 11 makes CoreCLR the default runtime across its platforms. The move to CoreCLR brings mobile and desktop apps onto the same runtime foundation as other .NET workloads. On Apple platforms that don’t allow JIT compilation, the runtime uses precompiled code and the interpreter rather than generating native code at runtime.Native AOT is an opt-in publishing alternative; its Android support remains experimental. Review the platform-specific runtime guidance and test your app’s libraries when choosing a deployment model.The UI development loop gets attention, too:XAML Incremental Hot Reload is a  in .NET 11. It updates live instances of pages and controls, including supported structural edits, without requiring you to navigate away and back. It’s enabled by default in Debug builds.Implicit XAML namespace declarations remove the need to repeat the standard namespace declarations at the root of every file.Windows uses the new  handler by default, while Android Shell adopts a handler-based architecture.New mobile test templates and  device selection bring testing into the CLI workflow.These are meaningful runtime and handler changes, so exercise navigation, custom handlers, and trimmed publishing as part of your upgrade. The optional Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Compatibility package no longer ships; apps that still reference it need to migrate off it..NET MAUI follows its own support policy, separate from the .NET runtime’s STS and LTS lifecycle.Windows: WinUI, Windows Forms, and WPFFor new Windows desktop apps, WinUI 3 is the recommended native UI framework, pairing C# and XAML with modern controls and the Fluent Design System. It’s delivered through the Windows App SDK, which also makes modern Windows APIs available to existing WPF and Windows Forms apps.Windows Forms in .NET 11 adds an opt-in modern visual styles mode that you can select at the application or control level. It also adds ways to react to system visual and accessibility settings, defer revealing a form until it’s ready, and suspend painting during bulk updates.These controls let you introduce refreshed rendering where it makes sense for your application instead of treating a framework upgrade as an automatic visual redesign.WPF updates focus on reliability, including fixes for Fluent window backdrops, window manager failures, and weak-event cleanup. Self-contained Windows publishing also includes a native dependency needed by apps that load mixed-mode WPF assemblies.Get started and plan your upgradeTo create and run a console app:dotnet new console -n HelloDotnet11 -f net11.0
dotnet run --project HelloDotnet11You can replace the generated program with either of the C# examples above to try the new language features.For an existing application, plan the upgrade across your development and deployment environments:Update the SDK selection in  if your repository pins it, and install the matching SDK on development machines and build agents.Update target frameworks to .NET 11, preserving platform-specific target framework suffixes where applicable. Update packages and workloads to compatible versions.Run your tests and measure the application under a representative workload. Validate your actual publishing mode, container base images, native dependencies, and deployment hosts.Upgrading from .NET 8 or .NET 9
.NET 8 and .NET 9 reach end of support on November 10, 2026. Move to a supported release to continue receiving fixes. Choose .NET 11 for the latest capabilities, or .NET 10 if its LTS lifecycle fits your requirements better. See the .NET support policy for dates and servicing requirements.
If you’re upgrading from a .NET 11 release candidate, update to the final SDK, runtime, and compatible package and workload versions rather than staying on a prerelease build.Thank you for building .NET with us.NET 11 is the work of . Thank you to everyone who contributed code, tested previews, reported issues, and helped us make better design decisions throughout the year. Your feedback across the previews and release candidates helped shape what we’re shipping today.The .NET ecosystem continues to grow, with more than  on NuGet and over 1.1 trillion package downloads.

This article describes new features in the .NET runtime for .NET 11.Updated minimum hardware requirementsThe minimum hardware requirements for .NET 11 have been updated to require more modern instruction sets on both x86/x64 and Arm64 architectures. Additionally, the ReadyToRun (R2R) compilation targets have been updated to take advantage of newer hardware capabilities.For Apple, there's no change to the minimum hardware or the ReadyToRun target. The Apple M1 chips are approximately equivalent to  and provide support for at least the  (NEON), , , , , , and  instruction sets.For Linux, there's no change to the minimum hardware. .NET continues to support devices such as Raspberry Pi that might only provide support for the  instruction set. The ReadyToRun target has been updated to include the  instruction set, which might result in additional jitting overhead if you launch an application.For Windows, the baseline is updated to require the  instruction set. This is required by Windows 11 and by all Arm64 CPUs officially supported by Windows 10. Additionally, it's inline with the Arm SBSA (Server Base System Architecture) requirements. The ReadyToRun target has been updated to be , which provides support for at least , , , , and , and covers the majority of hardware officially supported.For all three operating systems (Apple, Linux, and Windows), the baseline is updated from  to . This changes the hardware from only guaranteeing , , , and  to also guaranteeing , , , , , and . This guarantee is required by Windows 11 and by all x86/x64 CPUs officially supported on Windows 10. It includes all chips still officially supported by Intel and AMD, with the last older chips having gone out of support around 2013.The ReadyToRun target has been updated to  for Windows and Linux, which additionally includes the , , , , , , , and  instruction sets. The ReadyToRun target for Apple remains unchanged.Starting with .NET 11, .NET fails to run on older hardware and might print a message similar to the following:The current CPU is missing one or more of the baseline instruction sets.For ReadyToRun-capable assemblies, there might be additional startup overhead on some supported hardware that doesn't meet the expected support for a typical device..NET supports a broad range of hardware, often above and beyond the minimum hardware requirements put in place by the underlying operating system. This support adds significant complexity to the codebase, particularly for much older hardware that's unlikely to still be in use. Additionally, it defines a "lowest common denominator" that AOT targets must default to, which can, in some scenarios, lead to reduced performance.The update to the minimum baseline was made to reduce the maintenance complexity of the codebase and to better align with the documented (and often enforced) hardware requirements of the underlying operating system..NET 11 introduces runtime-native async (Runtime Async V2), a significant step toward replacing compiler-generated async state machines with runtime-managed suspension and resumption. Instead of the compiler emitting state-machine classes, the runtime itself tracks async execution, producing cleaner stack traces, better debuggability, and lower overhead.Runtime Async is a preview feature. To opt in, add the following property to your project file:<PropertyGroup>
  <Features>runtime-async=on</Features>
</PropertyGroup>
A  project no longer requires <EnablePreviewFeatures>true</EnablePreviewFeatures> to use Runtime Async.The .NET runtime libraries themselves are compiled with . The runtime libraries no longer contain compiler-generated state machines and rely entirely on runtime-provided async. This makes it possible to migrate an entire app (with only library dependencies) to the new model, and it provides broad functional and performance validation of the feature. The product team welcomes any reports—positive or negative—about throughput and library size changes you observe..NET 11 also includes two additional improvements:Covariant  →  overrides: When a derived class returns  for a base method that returns , the runtime now generates a void-returning thunk that bridges the calling convention difference, so virtual dispatch works for both flavors. The same fix applies to NativeAOT. Restrictions that prevented runtime-async methods from being inlined during ReadyToRun (R2R) compilation have been removed. All async tests pass with both crossgen2 and composite R2R, and inlining of await-less async calls (the synchronous fast path) is confirmed end-to-end.The  and  environment variables that previously controlled runtime-async behavior have been removed. To opt out of runtime-async per project, set <UseRuntimeAsync>false</UseRuntimeAsync> in your project file instead of relying on the environment variable.Cleaner live stack tracesThe most visible improvement is in —what profilers, debuggers, and  see during execution. With compiler-generated async, each async method produces multiple frames from state-machine infrastructure. With Runtime Async, the actual methods appear directly on the call stack.// To enable runtime async, add the following to your .csproj:
//   <Features>runtime-async=on</Features>

await OuterAsync();

static async Task OuterAsync()
{
    await Task.CompletedTask;
    await MiddleAsync();
}

static async Task MiddleAsync()
{
    await Task.CompletedTask;
    await InnerAsync();
}

static async Task InnerAsync()
{
    await Task.CompletedTask;
    Console.WriteLine(new StackTrace(fNeedFileInfo: true));
}
Without —13 frames, state-machine infrastructure visible:   at Program.<<Main>$>g__InnerAsync|0_2() in Program.cs:line 24
   at System.Runtime.CompilerServices.AsyncMethodBuilderCore.Start[TStateMachine](...)
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__InnerAsync|0_2()
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__MiddleAsync|0_1() in Program.cs:line 14
   at System.Runtime.CompilerServices.AsyncMethodBuilderCore.Start[TStateMachine](...)
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__MiddleAsync|0_1()
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__OuterAsync|0_0() in Program.cs:line 8
   at System.Runtime.CompilerServices.AsyncMethodBuilderCore.Start[TStateMachine](...)
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__OuterAsync|0_0()
   at Program.<Main>$(String[] args) in Program.cs:line 3
   at System.Runtime.CompilerServices.AsyncMethodBuilderCore.Start[TStateMachine](...)
   at Program.<Main>$(String[] args)
   at Program.<Main>(String[] args)
With —5 frames, the real call chain:   at Program.<<Main>$>g__InnerAsync|0_2() in Program.cs:line 24
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__MiddleAsync|0_1() in Program.cs:line 14
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__OuterAsync|0_0() in Program.cs:line 8
   at Program.<Main>$(String[] args) in Program.cs:line 3
   at Program.<Main>(String[] args)
Exception stack traces (from ) already look the same with or without Runtime Async, because existing  cleanup in compiler-generated code handles that case. The improvement is in what you see  live execution.This improvement benefits anything that inspects the live execution stack, including profiling tools, diagnostic logging, and the debugger call stack window.NativeAOT and ReadyToRun supportRuntime Async supports NativeAOT and ReadyToRun compilation. This extends the feature beyond JIT-compiled code to ahead-of-time compiled scenarios. The runtime also reuses continuation objects more aggressively and avoids saving unchanged locals, reducing allocation pressure in async-heavy code.Breakpoints now bind correctly inside runtime-async methods, and the debugger can step through  boundaries without jumping into compiler-generated infrastructure.Runtime Async includes several performance improvements: The JIT compiles a dedicated runtime-async version of a synchronous, task-returning method rather than delegating through a thunk. The JIT turns the method's tail calls into runtime-async calls and awaits the task that would otherwise be returned, eliminating an extra layer of indirection.Tail-merged suspension points: The JIT tail-merges async suspension points so generated code is smaller. Continuations used for runtime-async callable task thunks are cached and reused. Methods that are already pooled opt out of runtime-async, avoiding redundant work.Tiered async compilation: Async versions now flow through tiered compilation, so hot async methods get tier 1 optimizations after warm-up.Task and ValueTask factory intrinsics: The JIT recognizes common factories, such as , , and , and folds them into faster async paths.Tail-await optimizations: Implicit tailcalls from async methods are enabled again when the method directly returns another async result, and  avoids extra allocations in runtime-async paths.Async continuations without ExecutionContextAsync continuations can now opt out of  capture and restore.  carries ambient state—such as  values—across  points. Every  continuation previously captured a snapshot of the context and restored it before running, even when no  state was in use and the restore was a no-op.The runtime now detects when a continuation has nothing to restore and skips the capture/restore cycle entirely. , , , and  all benefit from this change, as does the runtime-async implementation path. Applications that use  and  sparingly see reduced overhead in high-throughput async code paths.Bounds check elimination: The just-in-time (JIT) compiler now eliminates bounds checks for the common pattern where an index plus a constant is compared against a length, such as . It also eliminates more redundant bounds checks for index-from-end access (for example, ). These improvements reduce redundant checks in tight loops and improve throughput for array and span operations.Redundant checked context removal: The JIT can now prove and remove redundant checked arithmetic contexts—for example, when a value is already known to be in range. This optimization eliminates unnecessary overflow checks in generated code.Switch expression folding: Multi-target  expressions now fold into simpler branchless checks when the targets are a small set of constants, for example x is 0 or 1 or 2 or 3 or 4.Faster uint-to-float/double casts: Casting  to  or  is faster on pre-AVX-512 x86 hardware. New Arm SVE2 (Scalable Vector Extension 2) intrinsics are available: ShiftRightLogicalNarrowingSaturate(Even|Odd). These expand the set of vectorized operations available on Arm hardware that supports SVE2.Math.BigMul(long, long, out long) is now significantly faster on x64. The JIT generates a single  instruction when both operands are 64-bit values and the caller requests the high half of the result, eliminating the previous helper call.Single-IG prolog restriction removed: The JIT no longer requires the function prolog to fit in a single instruction group (IG). Complex prologues with many saved registers, large stack allocations, or runtime-async state setup no longer trigger fallback paths. folding: The JIT now folds conditional selects whose two branches both produce the same constant into just that constant—for example,  becomes . This fold eliminates unnecessary comparisons that can appear after earlier optimizations unify branches.Saturating floating-point conversions: Unchecked  and  conversions to small integer types now saturate to type bounds instead of wrapping through intermediate truncation.ARM64  by reference for SVE: When the runtime is compiled with ARM SVE support,  values are passed by reference rather than by value, aligning with the ARM calling convention for scalable types and enabling better code generation for SVE-intensive code.For better performance and code quality, .NET 11 adds several more JIT optimizations:Constant-folding SequenceEqualThe JIT can now fold a  or ReadOnlySpan<T>.SequenceEqual call whose operands are both compile-time constants, replacing the byte-by-byte comparison with the constant  or  result. This matters most after inlining, when a caller passes another literal to a helper that compares against a known string. When  is inlined into a caller that passes , the JIT sees  and folds it to :static bool IsAdmin(string role) => role == "Admin";
The optimization applies to string literals,  fields, and UTF-8 literals (for example, ).Devirtualization improvementsThe JIT can now devirtualize generic virtual methods, including non-shared generic virtual methods and shared generic virtual methods that don't require a runtime lookup. Default interface methods declared on generic interfaces can now be devirtualized as well.In addition, the result of Activator.CreateInstance<T>() is now treated as having an exact type, allowing the JIT to devirtualize subsequent calls made on the returned object.These improvements unlock further downstream optimizations such as inlining and constant folding, reducing runtime dispatch overhead and improving performance. For example:IfaceGeneric<string> caller = new Caller<string>();
Iface obj = Activator.CreateInstance<Impl>();
caller.DefaultImpl(obj, "test"); // Can be optimized down to Console.WriteLine("test")

interface Iface
{
    void GenericMethod<T>(T value);
}

interface IfaceGeneric<T>
{
    void DefaultImpl(Iface obj, T value) => obj.GenericMethod(value);
}

class Impl : Iface
{
    public void GenericMethod<T>(T value) => Console.WriteLine(value);
}

class Caller<T> : IfaceGeneric<T>
{
}
Here the JIT can determine the exact implementation behind both interface calls, devirtualize them, inline through the call chain, and ultimately fold the code down to the equivalent of Console.WriteLine("test").Eliminating bounds checks after an empty-span guardThe JIT now picks up the  assertion from an empty-span check and uses it to prove the bounds check on the first element succeeds:if (!span.IsEmpty && span[0] == value)
{
    // The bounds check on span[0] is now eliminated.
}
Redundant branch and test eliminationWhen an outer predicate is already implied by an inner branch, the JIT now removes the redundant outer check. Similarly, when code assigns a value in a conditional and then immediately tests it, the second test is eliminated:if (x > 0)
{
    if (x > 1) S();  // The outer x > 0 check is folded away.
}

int y = condition ? 1 : 2;
if (y == 1) A(); else B();  // The y == 1 test is eliminated;
                             // each branch of the ternary goes directly to A() or B().
These optimizations are most visible after inlining, where guards from different methods land in the same compiled body.Hardware intrinsics and code generation.NET 11 includes several new hardware intrinsics and code generation improvements:Hardware FP16 instructions for  arithmetic and conversions: The JIT uses hardware FP16 instructions for Half arithmetic and conversions when the processor supports them. On x64, arithmetic uses AVX10.1, while conversions between  and  can use F16C (available on most AVX2-capable hardware). On Arm64, arithmetic uses the optional FP16 instruction set, while conversions between  and  or  use baseline Arm64 instructions. The optimization requires no application changes and preserves the existing ABI representation of .Better cost modeling for x86/x64 SIMD: The JIT's floating-point execution and size costs previously reflected x87-era assumptions. Updated costs that reflect modern SSE/AVX hardware let the JIT make better decisions about hoisting and common subexpression elimination (CSE) around SIMD code. Lowering for -style operations now emits a  sequence instead of / when AVX is available, which is consistently faster.Faster  on Arm64: Arm64 versions of , , and  no longer route through ExtractMostSignificantBits, yielding a 5–50% improvement in workloads that use these APIs in their core loop.Arm64  for 64-bit integers: on  and  now uses  instead of , which is shorter and faster on most cores.SVE  API expansion: gains signed, , and  variants alongside the existing unsigned ones, completing the predicate-generation surface for SVE loops.New SVE2 and Arm64 instruction-set detection: The runtime now reports , , , , and  as separate instruction sets, allowing  queries for those features on capable hardware. The CPUID-bit cache for AVX10 was being overwritten by a later query, which could cause AVX10 to be misreported on capable hardware. This is now fixed. and EqualityComparer<T>.Default are now specialized in ReadyToRun (R2R) images. Previously, the default comparers used reflection that R2R couldn't see ahead of time, causing callers to fall back to the JIT. R2R now generates a specialized helper in the image—mirroring the NativeAOT approach—and benchmarks show up to a 20× improvement for collections operations that rely on the default comparer.Cached interface dispatch on non-JIT platforms: On platforms that lack JIT support, such as iOS, interface dispatch was falling back to an expensive generic fixup path. Cached dispatch yields up to 200x improvements in interface-heavy code on these targets.Guid.NewGuid() on Linux now uses the  syscall with batch caching instead of reading from , yielding approximately 12% throughput improvement for GUID generation.Browser and WebAssembly support has several improvements: The runtime can now load WebCIL payloads directly, improving compatibility with browser-based deployment scenarios.Better debugging symbols: Symbol and stack trace quality for WebAssembly debugging has improved, making it easier to diagnose issues in browser-hosted .NET apps., , and  marshaling:, , and  are now marshaled more directly across JavaScript boundaries, reducing overhead for interop-heavy code..NET includes improvements to CoreCLR-on-WebAssembly:WebCIL V1 is the default for CoreCLR WASM builds: The shared WebCIL header gains a  field (28 → 32 bytes). Both Mono and CoreCLR readers accept V0 and V1. Crossgen2's  produces V1 directly, and CoreCLR-flavored WASM SDK builds default  to .Native re-link works for CoreCLR WASM apps: A full Emscripten-based pipeline replaces the previous stub targets. Re-linking  from the runtime pack and including custom native code via  now works.JavaScript minification in Release builds: Browser CoreCLR Release builds ship minified JavaScript.NativeAOT publish for WASM no longer drops package satellites: Satellite assemblies from NuGet packages are now passed to ILC and pruned from the publish output, fixing localization for AOT-published apps that depend on packages such as .CoreCLR on WebAssembly now runs the libraries test suite end to end.The .NET runtime can now initialize on machines with more than 1024 logical processors. Previously,  was called with the default  (capped at 1024), causing initialization to fail on high-core-count servers. The runtime now allocates the CPU set dynamically. The GC retains its 1024-heap limit, but the CPU count limit is removed.In-process crash report loggingA new in-process crash reporting mechanism captures diagnostic information from within the crashing process before it terminates. Previously, crash diagnostics were collected by an out-of-process monitor. While the out-of-process approach is safe, it can miss information that's only available inside the dying process. The new in-process path logs the managed stack trace, module list, and key runtime state to a well-known path before the process exits.This capability originated on mobile platforms and is now also available on Linux and macOS. When  isn't enabled, set DOTNET_EnableCrashReport=1 or DOTNET_EnableCrashReportOnly=1 to select the in-process reporter. Existing  behavior remains in place when minidumps are enabled.NativeAOT: faster interface dispatchNativeAOT now uses a dispatch helper for interface method calls. Instead of a direct fat-pointer call sequence, the runtime routes interface dispatch through a shared helper that can be patched to the correct implementation after the call site warms up. This reduces the binary size of interface call sites and improves throughput on workloads with many interface method calls.Generic virtual method dispatch also uses the shared dispatch-cell infrastructure, which improves both monomorphic and polymorphic call performance. In addition, Assembly.GetCallingAssembly() now works on NativeAOT when stack trace data is available.System.Runtime.Intrinsics now includes lane construction and composition APIs for hardware vector types. The new APIs let you construct a vector from individually specified lanes and extract or reorder lanes between vectors. This enables precise, portable control over SIMD vector element placement without falling back to platform-specific intrinsics.The new methods fall into a few families:, CreateAlternatingSequence, and  build a vector from a starting value and a rule.Interleave and de-interleave:, /, , /. The  family (, , , ) and .using System.Runtime.Intrinsics;

// {1, 2, 4, 8} — each lane is the previous lane times two
Vector128<int> powers = Vector128.CreateGeometricSequence(1, 2);

// Interleave two vectors lane-by-lane
(Vector128<int> lower, Vector128<int> upper) =
    Vector128.Zip(Vector128.Create(1), Vector128.Create(2));
These APIs are available on , , , , and . They're building blocks for image processing, audio digital signal processing (DSP), and other SIMD-intensive workloads that need fine-grained control over vector element layout.File locking on iOS and tvOSOn iOS and tvOS, including simulators, CoreCLR now disables advisory file locking by default. A FileStream that acquired an advisory lock could cause the operating system to terminate an app when the app entered the background while it held the lock. As a result, FileShare restrictions are no longer enforced by those locks on iOS and tvOS, including between handles in the same process. Mac Catalyst and other platforms keep their existing defaults.If your app relies on  enforcement and you accept the risk of suspension-related termination, restore file locking by using either of the following settings:Set the DOTNET_SYSTEM_IO_DISABLEFILELOCKING environment variable to .Set the System.IO.DisableFileLocking runtime configuration property to . For example, in the  file:{
  "configProperties": {
    "System.IO.DisableFileLocking": false
  }
}
The runtime includes fixes for the following issues:On Linux, the SDK no longer crashes on Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 8 on Arm64, and the runtime correctly traverses cgroup v2 memory limits at the root of the hierarchy.TryDequeue no longer spuriously reports an empty queue while a segment freezes during growth. TryPeek has the same reliability fix.On Windows, garbage collection works correctly on machines with multiple CPU groups.The async profiler now instruments both runtime-async methods and compiler-generated async state-machine methods, so tools receive one consistent event model regardless of async implementation style. The runtime also adds improved interpreter perf-map logging support, and fixes parsing of CORECLR_NOTIFICATION_PROFILERS lists that don't end with a semicolon.
